        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.5" n="5" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">ysse <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">ysse on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.6" n="6" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">le <hi rend="italic">auf Rasur</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">le on an erasure</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.7" n="7" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">se ðe <hi rend="italic">im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. übergeschr.</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">se ðe written above in the 16th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.8-3" n="8" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">þam <hi rend="italic">im</hi> 16. <hi rend="italic">Jh. eingefügt</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">þam inserted in the 16th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.9-3" n="9" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">später geänd. in</hi> þa<hi rend="italic">m</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">later altered to þa<hi rend="italic">m</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.oe.10-3" n="10" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">70</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">on <hi rend="italic">später</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">on later</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">S. Gl</hi> Ungehorsam 5. 16; Gefolge 19c; Finanz. Ælfred meint, der Zweck des Senats war, alle Fahrhabe des Römervolks, ausser Vieh, aus Steuer oder Beute unter Einem Dache für alle Freien gemeinsam nützlich zu verwalten (zu <hi rend="italic">Oros.</hi> ed. Sweet 72): er kennt also zentralen Staatsschatz</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">See Gl</hi> Ungehorsam 5. 16; Gefolge 19c; Finanz. Ælfred thinks that the purpose of the senate was to administer all the movable property of the Roman people, except cattle, from taxation or booty, under one roof for the common benefit of all freemen (at <hi rend="italic">Oros.</hi> ed. Sweet 72): he thus knows of a central state treasury</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Ich ändere Wortstellung, damit Fiskus auch für zweite Grafschaft als Empfänger erscheine</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">I alter the word order so that the fisc appears as recipient for the second shire as well</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> Grafschaft 11a. 18a; Pacht 11. Der Anteil des Ealdorman (<hi rend="italic">s. Gl</hi> 22b) am Strafgeld braucht nicht schon gerade der allerdings sicher Vor-Normannische Dritte Pfennig (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi>) zu sein</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> Grafschaft 11a. 18a; Pacht 11. The ealdorman’s share (<hi rend="italic">see Gl</hi> 22b) of the fine need not necessarily already be the Third Penny (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi>), though that is admittedly certainly pre-Norman</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">bessere Übs.: ‘wo er bisher Vassall war’ (‘und’ fügt nur H zu)</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">better translation: ‘where he was hitherto a vassal’ (only H adds ‘and’)</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_37-1.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">58</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">3</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Das Relativ <hi rend="italic">þe</hi> hängt vom folgenden <hi rend="italic">on</hi> ab. — ‘Dann’ fügt zu H cor.</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">The relative <hi rend="italic">þe</hi> depends on the following <hi rend="italic">on</hi>. — H cor. adds ‘then’</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.fn.11" n="11" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope></bibl><label>cyninge</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">(þam) cyninge</hi>. H*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.fn.12" n="12" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope></bibl><label>in</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">on</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.fn.13" n="13" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope></bibl><label>⁊</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">H*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.fn.14" n="14" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">80</biblScope></bibl><label>oncymð</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">(þonne) oncymð</hi>. H*.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.n.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">198</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Liebermann notes the growth of the authority of the state in this respect, as compared with Ine, cap. 39.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="endnote" xml:id="Af_37-1.attnote.n.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">198</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">Lit. ‘serving.’</seg></note>
      </div>
